<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latakia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laodicea ad Mare</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latakia is the main port city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast near the ruins of ancient Ugarit. Founded as Laodicea by the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> around 300 BCE, it was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the crusader Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. From 1920 to 1936 it was the capital of the French-mandate Alawite State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latakia.jpg" alt="Latakia" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Goodine · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latakia_downtown_view_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Latakia is the principal port city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the capital of the Latakia Governorate, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. It stands on the Ras Ziyarah promontory, which has a long history of occupation: a Phoenician city called Ramitha was located there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> named the Greek city Laodikeia on the Coast in honour of his mother, Laodice, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> the name became Laodicea ad Mare. Strabo described it as a most beautifully built city with a good harbour, whose mountain slopes were covered with vines that supplied much of the wine drunk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> conquered the city, along with most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in the 1st century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> declared it a free polis. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> rewarded it with the title of Metropolis in the 2nd century CE, exempted it from taxation, fortified it and for a few years made it the capital of Roman Syria. It fell to Muslim rule in the 7th century, when the general Ubadah ibn al-Samit attacked it during the conquest of Syria.</p><p>From 750 to 968 the city lost importance because it lay on the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. The Byzantines under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> recaptured it in 970, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> took it in 980. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> took it in 1086, and during the First Crusade Raymond of Saint-Gilles captured it in 1098. It was later ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-dabiq">Battle of Marj Dabiq</a> in 1516.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it had only about 1,400 to 1,700 inhabitants in the 16th century. From the late 17th century the Ibn al-Matarji family ruled it, and the growth of the tobacco industry helped the town to develop rapidly in the 18th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the city was assigned to the French mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and became the capital of the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alawites">Alawites</a>, the Alawite State, whose creation has often been interpreted as a divide and rule strategy against anti-colonial nationalism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Little of the classical city survives, many of its buildings having been destroyed by earthquakes. A Roman triumphal arch with four entrances and the Colonnade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a> remain, and the National Museum, in a 16th-century Ottoman caravanserai, holds inscribed tablets from Ugarit. The Port of Latakia, begun in 1950, is the main seaport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latakia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ugarit stood on the mound of Ras Shamra, about a kilometre from the Mediterranean north of modern <b>Latakia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1920 France established the Territory of the Alawites, covering the coastal mountains and the port of <b>Latakia</b>, where the Alawites, a heterodox Shia community long marginalised under the Ottomans, were the majority in the countryside.</blockquote><a href="/state/alawite-state">Alawite State</a></li><li><blockquote>Antioch's princes ruled a population of Greek Orthodox, Armenians, Syriac Christians and Muslims, with Italian merchants in <b>Latakia</b> and the port of Saint Symeon.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a></li></ul></section>
